requirement:
[p165]       7.1.     COLLAB-001 Contextual Collaboration
[p165] 
[p165] 
[p165] Users must be able to collaborate within a Pricing Initiative.
[p165] 
[p165] Collaboration may be attached to:
[p165] 
[p165]    ● the overall initiative;
[p165]    ● pricing scope;
[p165]    ● analysis;
[p165]    ● scenarios;
[p165]    ● pricing logic;
[p165]    ● proposals;
[p165]    ● review activity;
[p165]    ● execution or monitoring records.
[p165] 
[p165] Users should be able to understand what object or decision context a contribution refers to.
[p165] 
[p165]

[p166]       7.5.    COLLAB-005 Assignments
[p166] 
[p166] 
[p166] Authorised users must be able to assign specific actions to relevant participants.
[p166] 
[p166] Assignments may include tasks such as:
[p166] 
[p166]    ● provide cost information;
[p166]    ● review assumptions;
[p166]    ● validate commercial input;
[p166]    ● review a scenario;
[p166]    ● provide supporting evidence;
[p166]    ● complete review;
[p166]    ● implement an authorised pricing action;
[p166]    ● record monitoring information.
[p166] 
[p166] Each assignment should contain:
[p166] 
[p166]    ● assigned user;
[p166]    ● action required;
[p166] 
[p167]    ● associated initiative or pricing object;
[p167]    ● status;
[p167]    ● due date where applicable;
[p167]    ● creator;
[p167]    ● creation timestamp.
[p167]

[p167]       7.7.     COLLAB-007 Supporting Evidence
[p167] 
[p167] 
[p167] Users must be able to attach Evidence supporting or challenging pricing work.
[p167] 
[p167] Evidence is supporting material, observation or externally supplied information used to
[p167] inform, substantiate or question pricing work. Evidence does not become authoritative
[p167] structured Pricing Context merely because it is attached to a Pricing Initiative, analysis,
[p167] Scenario or Pricing Proposal.
[p167] 
[p167] Supported Evidence may include:
[p167] 
[p167]    ● uploaded documents;
[p167]    ● spreadsheets or extracts;
[p167]    ● images;
[p167]    ● internal reports;
[p167]    ● external links;
[p167]    ● manually entered observations;
[p167]    ● supplier information;
[p167]    ● competitor observations;
[p167]    ● customer or sales feedback;
[p167]    ● other relevant supporting material.
[p167] 
[p167] Evidence must remain attached or referenced to the pricing work it supports.
[p167] 
[p168] Evidence may support:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● an Assumption;
[p168]     ● business rationale;
[p168]     ● analytical interpretation;
[p168]     ● a proposed pricing action;
[p168]     ● a review or organisational decision;
[p168]     ● another supported decision-relevant element.
[p168] 
[p168] Evidence must remain distinguishable from:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● structured Pricing Context governed by Section 3.2;
[p168]     ● explicit Assumptions;
[p168]     ● deterministic calculations;
[p168]     ● AI-generated interpretation;
[p168]     ● comments or discussion.
[p168] 
[p168] Where information originating from Evidence is intentionally converted into structured
[p168] Owómidé Pricing Context, the resulting structured record must satisfy the applicable Section

[p168]       7.8.       COLLAB-008 Evidence Metadata
[p168] 
[p168] 
[p168] Evidence records should preserve, where available:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● title or description;
[p168]     ● contributor;
[p168]     ● submission date;
[p168]     ● source;
[p168]     ● related pricing object;
[p168]     ● observation date or effective date where relevant.
[p168] 
[p168] Users must be able to distinguish evidence from structured system data and AI-generated
[p168] interpretation.
[p168]

[p169]       7.9.     COLLAB-009 Manual Market Observations
[p169] 
[p169] 
[p169] Because relevant pricing intelligence may originate outside digitised systems, authorised
[p169] users must be able to record manually gathered market observations.
[p169] 
[p169] An observation should support fields such as:
[p169] 
[p169]     ● observation type;
[p169]     ● subject or competitor;
[p169]     ● observed value or price where applicable;
[p169]     ● location;
[p169]     ● channel;
[p169]     ● observation date;
[p169]     ● source or method;
[p169]     ● notes;
[p169]     ● submitting user.
[p169] 
[p169] Such observations must not be represented as automatically verified or real-time
[p169] intelligence.
[p169] 
[p169]

[p169]      7.10.     COLLAB-010 Evidence Relevance
[p169] 
[p169] 
[p169] Users should be able to associate evidence with the specific part of pricing work it informs.
[p169] 
[p169] For example, evidence may support:
[p169] 
[p169]     ● an assumption;
[p169]     ● competitor comparison;
[p169]     ● proposed price;
[p169]     ● cost change;
[p169]     ● commercial rationale;
[p169]     ● scenario;
[p169]     ● organisational decision.
[p169] 
[p169] This prevents the initiative from becoming an unstructured attachment repository.
[p169] 
[p169]

[p170]     7.13.       COLLAB-013 Collaboration Activity
[p170] 
[p170] 
[p170] Relevant collaboration events must appear in the Pricing Initiative activity history.
[p170] 
[p170] This includes:
[p170] 
[p170]    ● comments;
[p170]    ● replies;
[p170]    ● mentions;
[p170]    ● assignments;
[p170]    ● assignment completion;
[p170]    ● evidence additions;
[p170]    ● review feedback;
[p170] 
[p171]    ● issue resolution.
[p171]

[p218]     10.1.      AI-001 Contextual AI Assistance
[p218] 
[p218] 
[p218] AI assistance must be available within relevant pricing contexts, including:
[p218] 
[p218]    ● Pricing Initiatives;
[p218]    ● Pricing Builder;
[p218]    ● analysis and scenarios;
[p218]    ● proposals;
[p218]    ● review;
[p218]    ● collaboration;
[p218]    ● historical pricing work.
[p218] 
[p218] The AI should inherit the context of the object from which it is invoked.
[p218] 
[p218]

[p219]      10.3.     AI-003 Permission Inheritance
[p219] 
[p219] 
[p219] AI must operate within the permissions of the user invoking it.
[p219] 
[p219] The AI must not access, infer or expose information the user is not authorised to view.
[p219] 
[p219] This includes restrictions relating to:
[p219] 
[p219]     ● initiatives;
[p219]     ● organisational scope;
[p219]     ● cost information;
[p219]     ● sensitive financial information;
[p219]     ● historical pricing records;
[p219]     ● governance configuration.
[p219]

[p219]      10.5.     AI-005 Pricing Context Interpretation
[p219] 
[p219] 
[p219] Users must be able to ask AI to help interpret available pricing context.
[p219] 
[p219] AI may assist with:
[p219] 
[p219]     ● summarising the pricing situation;
[p219]     ● explaining relevant historical context;
[p219]     ● identifying important available inputs;
[p219]     ● highlighting missing information;
[p219]     ● identifying conflicting information;
[p219] 
[p220]    ● explaining the relationship between pricing inputs and the current initiative.
[p220]

[p220]     10.6.       AI-006 Pricing Work Construction
[p220] 
[p220] 
[p220] AI may assist users in developing pricing work.
[p220] 
[p220] This includes:
[p220] 
[p220]    ● drafting pricing strategies;
[p220]    ● converting business intent into structured pricing logic;
[p220]    ● drafting rules and conditions;
[p220]    ● suggesting parameters;
[p220]    ● identifying required assumptions;
[p220]    ● refining rationale;
[p220]    ● helping structure a Pricing Proposal.
[p220] 
[p220] AI-created material remains proposed content until explicitly applied by an authorised user.
[p220] 
[p220]

[p221]      10.8.     AI-008 Structured Output Validation
[p221] 
[p221] 
[p221] AI-generated structured pricing work must pass the same product validation applied to
[p221] human-created pricing work.
[p221] 
[p221] AI output must not bypass:
[p221] 
[p221]     ● required fields;
[p221]     ● formula validation;
[p221]     ● scope validation;
[p221]     ● guardrail evaluation;
[p221]     ● permissions;
[p221]     ● workflow rules.
[p221]

[p221]      10.9.     AI-009 Analysis Assistance
[p221] 
[p221] 
[p221] AI may assist users in interpreting pricing analysis.
[p221] 
[p221] It may:
[p221] 
[p221]     ● compare scenarios;
[p221]     ● explain financial implications;
[p221]     ● highlight material differences;
[p221]     ● identify unusual outcomes;
[p221]     ● surface trade-offs;
[p221]     ● identify entities requiring closer inspection;
[p221] 
[p222]    ● summarise guardrail results.
[p222]

[p223]    10.12.     AI-012 Risk and Inconsistency Detection
[p223] 
[p223] 
[p223] AI may identify potential issues including:
[p223] 
[p223]    ● contradictory assumptions;
[p223]    ● inconsistent rationale;
[p223]    ● missing supporting context;
[p223]    ● unusual pricing changes;
[p223]    ● incomplete proposal explanations;
[p223]    ● conflicts between stated objectives and proposed actions.
[p223] 
[p223] AI-generated risk identification must remain advisory unless the condition is independently
[p223] enforced by a product guardrail or validation rule.
[p223] 
[p223]

[p223]    10.14.     AI-014 Collaboration Assistance
[p223] 
[p223] 
[p223] AI may assist with collaborative pricing work by:
[p223] 
[p223]    ● summarising discussion;
[p223]    ● identifying unresolved questions;
[p223]    ● synthesising stakeholder input;
[p223]    ● identifying conflicting positions;
[p223]    ● summarising requested changes;
[p223]    ● preparing review summaries.
[p223] 
[p223] AI must not falsely attribute statements or decisions to users who did not make them.
[p223] 
[p223]

[p223]    10.15.     AI-015 Proposal Preparation
[p223] 
[p224] AI may assist users in preparing a Pricing Proposal by summarising:
[p224] 
[p224]    ● objective;
[p224]    ● proposed action;
[p224]    ● pricing scope;
[p224]    ● relevant economics;
[p224]    ● assumptions;
[p224]    ● guardrail status;
[p224]    ● supporting evidence;
[p224]    ● key risks;
[p224]    ● material changes.
[p224] 
[p224] The final submitted proposal must remain explicitly submitted by an authorised human user.
[p224] 
[p224]

[p225]    10.21.     AI-021 Uncertainty and Missing Basis
[p225] 
[p225] 
[p225] Where available information does not support a reliable conclusion, AI must indicate that
[p225] limitation rather than inventing missing pricing facts.
[p225] 
[p226] AI must not fabricate:
[p226] 
[p226]    ● competitor prices;
[p226]    ● costs;
[p226]    ● demand figures;
[p226]    ● margins;
[p226]    ● exchange rates;
[p226]    ● customer behaviour;
[p226]    ● approval authority;
[p226]    ● organisational policy.
[p226]

[p227]    10.27.      AI-027 AI and Guardrails
[p227] 
[p227] 
[p227] AI may assist users in understanding applicable organisational Guardrails.
[p227] 
[p227] Where permitted by the user's access, AI may explain or summarise:
[p227] 
[p227]    ● the Guardrail definition;
[p227]    ● applicable scope;
[p227]    ● configured threshold or condition;
[p227]    ● applicable configuration version;
[p227]    ● ANALYSIS-014 evaluation result;
[p227]    ● GOV-025 behavioural treatment;
[p227]    ● the effect of the Guardrail on Proposal or workflow progression.
[p227] 
[p227] AI must consume authoritative Guardrail information from the applicable product records.
[p227] 
[p227] AI must not independently:
[p227] 
[p227]    ● create an organisational Guardrail through ordinary pricing work;
[p227] 
[p228]    ● determine Guardrail applicability where governance has not established it;
[p228]    ● redefine a Guardrail;
[p228]    ● modify a Guardrail threshold, operator, scope or behavioural treatment;
[p228]    ● calculate a competing Guardrail result;
[p228]    ● replace an ANALYSIS-014 evaluation with AI reasoning;
[p228]    ● infer Near Threshold where the configured Guardrail does not define the required
[p228]        proximity rule;
[p228]    ● represent Unable to Evaluate as Within Guardrail;
[p228]    ● override a Guardrail;
[p228]    ● ignore a Blocking treatment;
[p228]    ● claim that an exception or authorised progression path exists where none has been
[p228]        configured.
[p228] 
[p228] AI may identify a possible need for a new or changed Guardrail as an advisory observation,
[p228] but that observation must not become organisational governance until an authorised user
[p228] completes the applicable governance action.
[p228] 
[p228] Where AI converts user pricing intent into structured Builder content, a user-defined pricing
[p228] boundary must remain an initiative-specific condition or constraint unless it references an
[p228] existing organisational Guardrail.
[p228] 
[p228] AI therefore assists with interpretation and explanation while Guardrail definition, evaluation
[p228] and behavioural consequence remain controlled by GOV-023, ANALYSIS-014 and GOV-025
[p228] respectively.
[p228] 
[p228]

[p229]    10.30.     AI-030 AI and Governance Administration
[p229] 
[p229] 
[p229] AI may explain existing workspace governance where the invoking user has access.
[p229] 
[p229] AI must not autonomously:
[p229] 
[p229]    ● grant permissions;
[p229]    ● assign commercial authority;
[p229]    ● modify approval limits;
[p229]    ● alter guardrails;
[p229]    ● activate policy autonomy;
[p229]    ● change workflow governance.
[p229]

[p262]      13.1.    NOTIF-001 Personal Work Queue
[p262] 
[p262] 
[p262] Every active user should have a personal work view containing pricing work that currently
[p262] requires or may require their attention.
[p262] 
[p262] This may include initiatives where the user is:
[p262] 
[p262]    ● owner;
[p262]    ● collaborator;
[p262]    ● reviewer;
[p262]    ● approver;
[p262]    ● assignee;
[p262]    ● implementation owner;
[p262]    ● mentioned;
[p262]    ● responsible for monitoring activity.
[p262]

[p262]      13.2.    NOTIF-002 Action Required Separation
[p262] 
[p262] 
[p262] The system must distinguish between information that is merely visible to a user and work
[p262] that requires an explicit action from that user.
[p262] 
[p262] Examples of action-required items include:
[p262] 
[p262]    ● assignment due;
[p262]    ● review requested;
[p262]    ● approval requested;
[p262]    ● requested clarification;
[p262]    ● execution assigned;
[p262]    ● monitoring input required.
[p262]

[p263]      13.3.     NOTIF-003 Workspace-Level Visibility
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] Authorised users must be able to view relevant organisation-level pricing activity.
[p263] 
[p263] The workspace should provide visibility into categories such as:
[p263] 
[p263]    ● active Pricing Initiatives;
[p263]    ● initiatives requiring attention;
[p263]    ● pending reviews;
[p263]    ● pending approvals;
[p263]    ● ready-for-execution work;
[p263]    ● implementation in progress;
[p263]    ● monitoring activity;
[p263]    ● recently completed initiatives.
[p263] 
[p263] Visibility must respect the user's role and scope.
[p263] 
[p263]

[p263]      13.4.     NOTIF-004 Pricing Initiative Status Visibility
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] Pricing Initiative listings must display enough information for users to understand current
[p263] state without opening every initiative.
[p263] 
[p263] Relevant information should include:
[p263] 
[p263]    ● initiative title;
[p263]    ● initiative type;
[p263]    ● owner;
[p263]    ● pricing scope summary;
[p263]    ● lifecycle state;
[p263]    ● urgency;
[p263]    ● current responsible party;
[p263]    ● next required action;
[p263]    ● target or effective date where applicable.
[p263]

[p263]      13.5.     NOTIF-005 Notification Events
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] The MVP must generate notifications for consequential events where user awareness or
[p263] action is required.
[p263] 
[p263] These should include, where relevant:
[p263] 
[p264]    ● user assignment;
[p264]    ● mention;
[p264]    ● review request;
[p264]    ● approval request;
[p264]    ● changes requested;
[p264]    ● proposal rejection;
[p264]    ● proposal approval;
[p264]    ● proposal policy authorisation;
[p264]    ● escalation;
[p264]    ● upcoming due date;
[p264]    ● overdue action;
[p264]    ● execution assignment;
[p264]    ● implementation issue;
[p264]    ● monitoring assignment.
[p264]

[p264]     13.6.     NOTIF-006 Notification Recipient
[p264] 
[p264] 
[p264] Notifications must be sent only to users relevant to the event.
[p264] 
[p264] The system must determine recipients based on:
[p264] 
[p264]    ● assignment;
[p264]    ● workflow responsibility;
[p264]    ● role;
[p264]    ● scope;
[p264]    ● mention;
[p264]    ● ownership;
[p264]    ● organisational configuration.
[p264] 
[p264] A notification must never provide an unauthorised user access to protected pricing
[p264] information.
[p264] 
[p264]

[p266]    13.12.      NOTIF-012 Pending Approval Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Users with approval authority must have a clear view of proposals currently awaiting their
[p266] decision.
[p266] 
[p266] Each approval item should show relevant information such as:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● Pricing Initiative;
[p266]    ● proposal version;
[p266]    ● submitting user;
[p266]    ● submitted date;
[p266]    ● urgency;
[p266]    ● pricing scope;
[p266]    ● current workflow stage;
[p266]    ● approval deadline where applicable.
[p266]

[p266]    13.13.      NOTIF-013 Review Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Users responsible for reviews must be able to identify:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● review requests assigned to them;
[p266]    ● review status;
[p266]    ● due date where applicable;
[p266]    ● initiative urgency.
[p266]

[p266]    13.14.      NOTIF-014 Execution Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Authorised Executors must be able to identify pricing actions that are:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● Ready for Execution;
[p266] 
[p267]    ● assigned to them;
[p267]    ● partially implemented;
[p267]    ● blocked;
[p267]    ● overdue.
[p267]

[p267]    13.18.      NOTIF-018 Escalation Visibility
[p267] 
[p267] 
[p267] Where an escalation occurs, relevant users must be able to identify:
[p267] 
[p267]    ● what was escalated;
[p267]    ● why escalation occurred;
[p267] 
[p268]    ● previous responsible party;
[p268]    ● current responsible party;
[p268]    ● resulting action required.

[p269]    13.24.      NOTIF-024 Workspace Filtering
[p269] 
[p269] 
[p269] Workspace-level work views should support filtering by relevant dimensions such as:
[p269] 
[p269]    ● status;
[p269]    ● owner;
[p269]    ● initiative type;
[p269]    ● urgency;
[p269]    ● category;
[p269]    ● product;
[p269]    ● location;
[p269]    ● channel;
[p269]    ● responsible participant;
[p269]    ● date range.
[p269] 
[p269] Filtering must respect user access scope.
[p269] 
[p269]

[p269]    13.25.      NOTIF-025 Workspace Search
[p269] 
[p269] 
[p269] Users must be able to search pricing work they are authorised to access.
[p269] 
[p269] Search should support relevant identifiers such as:
[p269] 
[p269]    ● Pricing Initiative title;
[p269]    ● product or SKU;
[p269]    ● category;
[p269]    ● proposal identifier;
[p269]    ● owner.
[p269]
